### Neovim version (nvim -v)

e5a4f41da455cdb9b9aae0b3d5c752444eff6b33

### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?

No

### Operating system/version

Arch Linux

### Terminal name/version

nvim-qt

### $TERM environment variable

nvim-qt

### Installation

Git

### How to reproduce the issue

Extracted from https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/16431:

> I think I found an issue that may be related, which happens on both `master` and this PR. To reproduce, create a Rust file with this content:
>
> ```rust
> pub(crate) struct TypeParameter {
>     name: String,
>     requirements: Vec<()>,
> }
>
> impl TypeParameter {
>     pub(crate) fn new(name: String) -> Self {
>         Self { name, requirements: Vec::new() }
>     }
> }
>
> fn main() {}
> ```
>
> Then do the following:
>
> 1. Format and save the file using `exe 'lua vim.lsp.buf.formatting_sync(nil, 5000)' | w`
> 1. Undo the changes
> 1. Run `exe 'lua vim.lsp.buf.formatting_sync(nil, 5000)' | w` again
>
> In my setup, this results in the buffer being formatted like this:
>
> ```rust
> pub(crate) struct TypeParameter {
>     name: String,
>     requirements: Vec<()>,
> }
>
> impl TypeParameter {
>     pub(crate) fn new(name: String) -> Self {
>         Self {
>             name,
>            ,
>         name, requirements: Vec::new() }
>     }
> }
>
> fn main() {}
> ```
>
> This happens even with debouncing disabled. It also happens regardless of what `allow_incremental_sync` is set to. It doesn't happen when I run `rustfmt` on the file directly, so I don't think it's a rustfmt issue.

The buffer states of rust-analyzer when this happens:

> The rust-analyzer buffer states in this case are:
>
> **initial**:
>
> ```rust
> pub(crate) struct TypeParameter {
>     name: String,
>     requirements: Vec<()>,
> }
>
> impl TypeParameter {
>     pub(crate) fn new(name: String) -> Self {
>         Self { name, requirements: Vec::new() }
>     }
> }
>
> fn main() {}
> ```
>
> **First `exe` format**:
>
> ```rust
> pub(crate) struct TypeParameter {
>     name: String,
>     requirements: Vec<()>,
> }
>
> impl TypeParameter {
>     pub(crate) fn new(name: String) -> Self {
>         Self {
>             name,
>             requirements: Vec::new(),
>         }
>     }
> }
>
> fn main() {}
> ```
>
> **another undo followed by the `exe` format**:
>
> ```rust
> pub(crate) struct TypeParameter {
>     name: String,
>     requirements: Vec<()>,
> }
>
> impl TypeParameter {
>     pub(crate) fn new(name: String) -> Self {
>         Self { name, requirements: Vec::new() }
>     }
> }
>
> fn main() {}
> ```
>
> At this point however the buffer in NeoVim looks like this:
>
> ```rust
> pub(crate) struct TypeParameter {
>     name: String,
>     requirements: Vec<()>,
> }
>
> impl TypeParameter {
>     pub(crate) fn new(name: String) -> Self {
>         Self {
>             name,
>            ,
>         name, requirements: Vec::new() }
>     }
> }
>
> fn main() {}
> ```
>
> So something in NeoVim (I guess?) is resulting in a mismatch between what NeoVim
> is displaying, and what rust-analyzer thinks the buffer looks like.

